---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import {
  Button,
  Dialog,
  DialogContent,
  DialogFooter,
  DialogHeader,
  DialogTitle,
} from "opui-css/astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import StickyHeader from "../../component-examples/table/StickyHeader.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="table-scroll-state">
  <section id="the-problem">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      <code>position: sticky</code> keeps a table header in view, but the header looks
      the same whether it's resting at the top of the table or floating over the rows.
      A shadow shows that it's stuck, and so far that took a scroll listener or an
      <code>IntersectionObserver</code> with a sentinel element.
    </p>
    <p>
      A scrolling area inside a dialog has the same problem: nothing says that
      there's more content above or below the fold.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-trick">
    <h2>The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>container-type: scroll-state</code> on the sticky
        {" "}<code>&lt;thead&gt;</code> makes it a container that knows its scroll
        state
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>@container scroll-state(stuck: block-start)</code> matches while the
        header is stuck to the top edge. An element can't query itself, so the shadow
        goes on the <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> cells inside it
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>clip-path</code> cuts the shadow off at the top and the sides, so it
        only falls on the rows below
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.table.sticky-header > thead {
  container-type: scroll-state;
  inset-block-start: 0;
  position: sticky;
}

.table.sticky-header > thead th {
  transition: box-shadow 0.2s;
}

@container scroll-state(stuck: block-start) {
  .table.sticky-header > thead th {
    box-shadow: var(--ui-shadow-scroll);
    clip-path: inset(0 0 calc(-1 * var(--ui-space-6)));
  }
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="scroll-shadows">
    <h2>Scroll shadows</h2>
    <p>
      <DocLink href="/components/dialog">Dialog</DocLink> and <DocLink
        href="/components/drawer">Drawer</DocLink
      > use the other half of the feature. <code
        >scroll-state(scrollable: block-start)</code
      > matches when a scroll container can scroll up, so there's content above, and
      {" "}<code>scrollable: block-end</code> when there's more below.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The <code>.content</code> area is the scroll container. Its <code
          >::before</code
        > and <code>::after</code> are its children, so they can query it
      </li>
      <li>
        Children of a scroller scroll along with it. <code
          >position: absolute</code
        > takes them out of the flow, and <code>anchor()</code> pins them to the edges
        of the content area
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>anchor-scope</code> keeps the anchor name to one dialog, so two open
        dialogs don't share it
      </li>
      <li>
        Both shadows start at <code>opacity: 0</code>, and each query fades one
        in. <code>scale: 1 -1</code> flips the bottom one so its shadow falls upward
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.content {
  anchor-name: --content;
  anchor-scope: --content;
  container-type: scroll-state;
  overflow-block: auto;
}

.content::before,
.content::after {
  block-size: var(--ui-space-2);
  box-shadow: var(--ui-shadow-scroll);
  clip-path: inset(100% 0 calc(-1 * var(--ui-space-6)));
  content: "";
  inset-inline: anchor(--content inside);
  opacity: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  position: absolute;
  transition: opacity 0.2s;
}

.content::before {
  inset-block-end: anchor(--content outside);
}

.content::after {
  inset-block-start: anchor(--content outside);
  scale: 1 -1;
}

@container scroll-state(scrollable: block-start) {
  .content::before {
    opacity: 1;
  }
}

@container scroll-state(scrollable: block-end) {
  .content::after {
    opacity: 1;
  }
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="try-it">
    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>Scroll the table until the header sticks.</p>
    <Example showCode={false}>
      <StickyHeader />
    </Example>
    <p>Open the dialog and scroll its content.</p>
    <Example centered showCode={false}>
      <Button
        commandfor="scroll-state-dialog"
        command="show-modal"
        type="button"
      >
        Open dialog
      </Button>
      <Dialog
        closedby="any"
        id="scroll-state-dialog"
        style="max-block-size: 24rem"
      >
        <DialogHeader>
          <DialogTitle>Terms</DialogTitle>
        </DialogHeader>
        <DialogContent>
          <div class="ui-rich-text">
            {
              Array.from({ length: 8 }, (_, index) => (
                <p>
                  Section {index + 1}. The shadow at the top shows once you
                  scroll down, and the one at the bottom goes away at the end.
                </p>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </DialogContent>
        <DialogFooter>
          <Button
            commandfor="scroll-state-dialog"
            command="close"
            type="button"
            variant="filled"
          >
            Close
          </Button>
        </DialogFooter>
      </Dialog>
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section id="fallback">
    <h2>Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Without scroll state queries the header still sticks and the content
        still scrolls. There's just no shadow
      </li>
      <li>
        The shadows are decoration, so nothing else depends on them. A scrollbar
        still shows that there's more
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
